\description{
\strong{Everything is 0-based.} There is one rule, so nothing has to be converted per call
and there is no part of the package you have to remember an exception for:

\itemize{
\item \strong{Intervals are half-open \verb{[start, end)}} -- the BED convention. This covers the
bundled datasets (\link{PF3D7_GENES}, \link{PF_EXAMPLE_DRUG_GENES}, \link{PF3D7_CORE_REGIONS},
\link{PF3D7_PARALOG_GENES}), any \code{genes} track you supply, the IBD \code{blocks}, \verb{locus =}
arguments, and \code{\link[=bed_intersect]{bed_intersect()}}. \code{end - start} is the width in bp, and intervals
that merely touch (\code{end1 == start2}) do not overlap.
\item \strong{Variant positions are 0-based too}, including the \code{pos} column and the
\code{chr:pos} \code{snp_id} of the per-SNP tables, and the \verb{snps =} arguments that select
them. A variant sits inside a gene when \code{pos >= start & pos < end}.
}

Formats that number differently are converted once, at the boundary, and never leak
inward: the PlasmoDB GFF (1-based inclusive) is shifted where the gene datasets are
built in \verb{data-raw/}; \code{hmmibd-rs} blocks (0-based, both endpoints inclusive) get
\code{end + 1} when an \link{IbdResults} reads them; and VCF \code{POS} becomes \code{POS - 1} both in the
companion Python package before any table reaches R and in \code{\link[=load_genotypes]{load_genotypes()}}, which is
where positions enter on the R side (\pkg{SNPRelate} reports 1-based \code{POS}). That last
one matters more than it looks: a scan built from those genotypes -- \code{\link[=run_ihs]{run_ihs()}},
\code{\link[=beta_score]{beta_score()}}, \code{pop_diff_snps()} -- would otherwise sit one base off every IBD table and
every interval, which breaks exact joins between tracks and mis-assigns a SNP sitting on a
